from lessons import L

L(8, 'Decisions: if / else', '🔀', 25,
  'Make your program choose: ask yes/no questions with comparisons and run different code with if, elif and else.',
  ['I know a comparison gives True or False', 'I never mix up = (store) and == (ask)', 'I can write if / elif / else with a colon and 4-space indentation', 'I can combine tests with and, or, not and check even/odd with %'],
  [
  ('p', 'So far every program did the <b>same thing</b> every time. Real programs <b>decide</b>: "Is the user old enough?", "Is the password right?", "Did the player win?" To decide, Python first needs a <b>yes/no question</b>.'),
  ('h', '1. A comparison asks a yes/no question'),
  ('p', 'A <b>comparison</b> compares two values and gives back a <b>Boolean</b> (type <code>bool</code>). A Boolean has only two possible values: <code>True</code> or <code>False</code> (capital first letter, no quotes).'),
  ('show', 'print(5 > 3)\nprint(5 < 3)\nprint(type(5 > 3))', []),
  ('table', ['Operator', 'Asks', 'Example', 'Answer'], [
     ['<code>==</code>', 'equal to?', '<code>4 == 4</code>', 'True'],
     ['<code>!=</code>', 'NOT equal to?', '<code>4 != 4</code>', 'False'],
     ['<code>&lt;</code>', 'smaller than?', '<code>3 &lt; 5</code>', 'True'],
     ['<code>&gt;</code>', 'bigger than?', '<code>3 &gt; 5</code>', 'False'],
     ['<code>&lt;=</code>', 'smaller than or equal?', '<code>5 &lt;= 5</code>', 'True'],
     ['<code>&gt;=</code>', 'bigger than or equal?', '<code>4 &gt;= 5</code>', 'False']]),
  ('rule', 'One = stores. Two == ask.', '<code>x = 5</code> <b>puts</b> 5 into x. <code>x == 5</code> <b>asks</b> "is x equal to 5?" and answers True or False. A comparison never changes x. Inside an <code>if</code>, a single <code>=</code> is a SyntaxError.'),
  ('try', 'x = 5\nprint(x == 5)\nprint(x == 6)\nprint(x != 6)\nprint(x >= 5)', [], 'Predict all four answers first, then press Run. Notice that <code>x >= 5</code> is True when x is exactly 5.'),
  ('h', 'Numbers and text are not the same'),
  ('p', 'Comparing text with text works (it checks letter by letter, and capital letters count). But the <b>number 5</b> and the <b>text "5"</b> are different things:'),
  ('show', 'print("apple" == "apple")\nprint("Apple" == "apple")\nprint("5" == 5)\nprint(5 == 5.0)', []),
  ('trap', '<b>input() gives TEXT.</b> If you write <code>age = input()</code> and then <code>age >= 18</code>, Python crashes with a <b>TypeError</b> (text cannot be compared with a number using &gt;= or &lt;). Convert first: <code>age = int(input())</code>. Worse, text compares letter by letter, so <code>"10" &lt; "9"</code> is <b>True</b> because "1" comes before "9".'),
  ('try', 'age = input()\nprint(age >= 18)', ['20'], 'Run it and read the error. Then change the first line to <code>age = int(input())</code> and run again.'),
  ('h', '2. if: run code only when the answer is True'),
  ('p', 'An <code>if</code> checks a question. If the answer is <b>True</b>, the <b>indented</b> lines under it run. If it is <b>False</b>, they are <b>skipped</b>.'),
  ('branch', 'age >= 18', 'the indented lines run', 'the indented lines are skipped'),
  ('try', 'age = int(input())\nif age >= 18:\n    print("You can vote!")\nprint("Done")', ['20'], 'Run with 20, then with 12. Which line always prints, and why?'),
  ('rule', 'The two things every if needs', '1) A <b>colon :</b> at the end of the <code>if</code> line. 2) The lines inside are <b>indented</b> (4 spaces). Indentation is how Python knows what belongs to the if. The first line that is <b>not</b> indented is outside it and always runs.'),
  ('h', 'else: the other choice'),
  ('p', '<code>else</code> has no question. It runs when the <code>if</code> answer was False. Exactly <b>one</b> of the two parts runs.'),
  ('try', 'age = int(input())\nif age >= 18:\n    print("adult")\nelse:\n    print("minor")', ['18'], 'Try 18, 17 and 40. Then change <code>&gt;=</code> to <code>&gt;</code> and try 18 again. What changed?'),
  ('h', 'elif: more than two choices'),
  ('p', '<code>elif</code> means "otherwise, if...". Python checks from the top and runs <b>only the first branch whose answer is True</b>, then skips all the rest.'),
  ('trace', 'score = 85\nif score >= 90:\n    print("A")\nelif score >= 80:\n    print("B")\nelif score >= 70:\n    print("C")\nelse:\n    print("Try again")', []),
  ('trap', '<b>Order matters!</b> Put the hardest test first. If you write <code>if score &gt;= 60</code> first, a score of 95 is caught right there and prints C. The later tests never get a chance. Also: two separate <code>if</code>s are both checked, but <code>if</code> + <code>elif</code> runs only one.'),
  ('show', 'x = 10\nif x > 5:\n    print("A")\nif x > 8:\n    print("B")\nprint("---")\nif x > 5:\n    print("A")\nelif x > 8:\n    print("B")', []),
  ('h', '3. and, or, not'),
  ('p', 'Join two questions into one with <code>and</code> / <code>or</code>, or flip an answer with <code>not</code>.'),
  ('table', ['Left', 'Right', 'Left and Right', 'Left or Right', 'not Left'], [
     ['True', 'True', 'True', 'True', 'False'],
     ['True', 'False', 'False', 'True', 'False'],
     ['False', 'True', 'False', 'True', 'True'],
     ['False', 'False', 'False', 'False', 'True']]),
  ('rule', 'and = BOTH. or = AT LEAST ONE.', '<code>age &gt;= 13 and age &lt;= 19</code> is True only when <b>both</b> parts are True (a teenager). <code>age &lt; 5 or age &gt; 65</code> is True when <b>either</b> part is True. Write each question in full: <code>age &gt; 5 and &lt; 10</code> is a SyntaxError.'),
  ('try', 'age = int(input())\nif age < 5 or age > 65:\n    print("free ticket")\nelse:\n    print("pay full price")', ['70'], 'Try 70, 30, 4 and the borders 5 and 65. Then change <code>or</code> to <code>and</code>: does anybody ever get a free ticket now?'),
  ('h', '4. The remainder operator %'),
  ('p', 'Two new math symbols. <code>//</code> divides and keeps only the <b>whole part</b>. <code>%</code> gives what is <b>left over</b> (the remainder).'),
  ('show', 'print(17 // 5)\nprint(17 % 5)\nprint(10 % 2)\nprint(7 % 2)\nprint(2 % 5)', []),
  ('rule', 'Even or odd: n % 2 == 0', 'Dividing an even number by 2 leaves <b>nothing</b>, so <code>n % 2</code> is <b>0</b>. An odd number leaves <b>1</b>. In the same way <code>n % 3 == 0</code> means "n can be split into groups of 3 exactly".'),
  ('try', 'n = int(input())\nif n % 2 == 0:\n    print("even")\nelse:\n    print("odd")', ['7'], 'Try 7, 10 and 0.'),
  ('h', '5. An if inside an if'),
  ('p', 'You can put an <code>if</code> <b>inside</b> another one. Indent the inner part 4 more spaces (8 in all). The inner question is only asked when the outer one was True.'),
  ('show', 'n = 15\nif n > 10:\n    print("over 10")\n    if n > 20:\n        print("over 20")\nprint("end")', []),
  ('h', 'Mistakes and the errors they cause'),
  ('table', ['Mistake', 'What Python says'], [
     ['<code>if x &gt; 3</code> (no colon)', 'SyntaxError'],
     ['<code>if x = 5:</code> (one = in a test)', 'SyntaxError'],
     ['<code>else:</code> with no if before it', 'SyntaxError'],
     ['The line under <code>if</code> is not indented', 'IndentationError'],
     ['An extra indent on a normal line', 'IndentationError'],
     ['Two lines in one block indented by different amounts', 'IndentationError'],
     ['<code>age = input()</code> then <code>age &gt;= 18</code>', 'TypeError'],
     ['<code>"5" == 5</code>', 'No error. Just False.']]),
  ('quick', 'x = 8. What does print(x % 3 == 2) show?', ['True', 'False', '2', 'Error'], 0, '8 % 3 is 2 (3 fits twice with 2 left), and 2 == 2 is True.'),
  ('quick', 'score = 75 and the chain is: if score >= 70 / elif score >= 60 / else. How many branches run?', ['One: the if', 'Two: the if and the elif', 'All three', 'None'], 0, '75 >= 70 is True, so the first branch runs and the rest are skipped.'),
  ('quick', 'Which line is correct Python?', ['if x == 5:', 'if x = 5:', 'if x == 5', 'if (x == 5)'], 0, 'You need two equal signs to ask, and a colon at the end.'),
  ])
